 "result": "Three of the 24 failures are not caused by the `partitioned` TypeError.\n\n1. **`tests/test_helpers.py::TestStreaming::test_async_view`**: `asgiref` isn't installed, so `Tessel.async_to_sync` (`src/tessel/app.py:1096`) raises `RuntimeError: Install Tessel with the 'async' extra`. This is a missing optional dependency, and I confirmed it from the traceback.\n\n2. **`tests/test_request.py::test_limit_config`**: `Request({}).max_form_memory_size` is `None`, but the test expects Werkzeug's default of `500_000` when there is no app context (`tests/test_request.py:33`). This is probably the same Werkzeug 3.0.3 vs 3.1 mismatch, since that attribute looks like a 3.1 addition. It is a different failure from the cookie TypeError. I did not read the Tessel `Request` source or the Werkzeug 3.0.3 source to confirm that, so that link is an inference.\n\n3. **`tests/test_helpers.py::test_open_resource_exceptions[a]`**: It failed once in the first full run with a pytest-level error (`pytest.Pytes...`, truncated in the output). It passed on every later run, including the full `-v` run and in isolation. I treat it as a flaky failure, probably triggered by leaked state from the failing session tests, but I didn't capture the full error, so I can't say exactly why.\n\nThe one **error** (teardown of `test_stream_keeps_session`) is not a separate test failure. It is a knock-on of the main cause, because the TypeError left an app context pushed and the `leak_detector` fixture caught it.\n\nThe other 21 failures all trace directly to the `partitioned` TypeError at `src/tessel/sessions.py:374`.",
